<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="cyanidin 3,7-diglucoside polyacylation biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7464" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The occurrence of 7-polyacylated anthocyanins as the main pigments inducing blue color(s) in flowers has been reported from various plant families |CITS:[19554240]|. Many of them are delphinidin derivatives which have been found in the &lt;i&gt;Campanulaceae&lt;/i&gt; family, for instance |FRAME:CPD-16561| (see |FRAME:PWY-7458|) |CITS:[Kondo90] [7763601]|. The formation of this pigment, i.e. |FRAME:CPD-16561| is different from the anthocyanin 3,5-glucoside derivatives, for instance found in the &lt;i&gt;Gentianaceae&lt;/i&gt; family (see |FRAME:PWY-5307|) where |FRAME:CPD-7965| fulfills the same function as |FRAME:CPD-16561| in conferring blue flower colors to this species |CITS:[Goto82]|. The formation of 7-polyacylated cyanidins (this pathway) has its beginning with |FRAME:CPD-15005| which has been formed as a crucial compound before |CITS:[20971893] [22673029]| (see |FRAME:PWY-7256|). The progression of the |FRAME:PWY-7464| has been elucidated by enzymes isolated from sepals of &lt;i&gt;Delphinium grandiflorum&lt;/i&gt; assayed with cyanidin 3,7-diglucoside derivative intermediates deduced from the delphinidin 3-rutinoside 7-diglucosides of the |FRAME:PWY-7458|. The noteworthiness of this pathway is the utilization of one acyl-glucose donor, i.e. |FRAME:CPD-15283| by both glucosyltransferases and acetyltransferases which further furnish the cyanidin 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-side chain. The donor provides both glucosyl- and p-hydroxybenzoyl groups and in doing so serves as a zwitter donor in the |FRAME:PWY-7464| |CITS:[24179131]|. The two acylation steps in the pathway, i.e. the conversion of |FRAME:CPD-15005| to |FRAME:CPD-16602| and |FRAME:CPD-16603| to |FRAME:CPD-16604| have been demonstrated in crude extracts from sepals of &lt;i&gt;Delphinium grandiflorum&lt;/i&gt;. Although the cDNA (SCPL2) for the acyl-glucose dependent anthocyanin glucoside(s) acyltransferase was obtained the expression in an recombinant organism failed but its molecular function could be confirmed &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; in white &lt;i&gt;Delphinium&lt;/i&gt; varieties where the gene was absent |CITS:[24179131]|. Two cDNA&apos;s encoding for the acyl-glucose dependent anthocyanin 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-(6-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-(p-hydroxybenzoyl)-glucoside) glucosyltransferase catalyzing the conversion of |FRAME:CPD-16602| to |FRAME:CPD-16603| were also isolated, expressed in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; and biochemically identified. Both, glucosyltransferase and acyltransferase were localized to the vacuole which coincides with the localization of |FRAME:CPD-16561| |CITS:[24179131]| and the occurrence of other enzymes of the pathway |CITS:[20971893] [22673029]|.</Description>
